ct with gas-phase reagents. That’s not a trick; it’s real chemistry on a single atom. ### 3. Many “natural” elements are just as fleeting Your body contains carbon-14 (half-life 5,730 years—short by geological standards). Radium, discovered by Marie Curie, has an isotope with a half-life of only 3.6 days. Francium (element 87) is so unstable its most stable isotope has a half-life of 22 minutes; any francium that existed when Earth formed decayed eons ago. Yet no one would say radium or francium are “fake” elements. Superheavy elements are just the extreme end of that same spectrum: their nuclear structure fights a losing battle against electrostatic repulsion, but for a brief moment they are intact. ### 4. A better analogy: a hummingbird’s wingbeat A soap bubble is a fragile, macroscopic structure that pops irreversibly into disordered droplets. A superheavy nucleus is more like a hummingbird in flight: its wings beat so fast (50 times a second) that you can’t see them, but if you freeze the motion with a high-speed camera, you capture a complete wing with shape, feathers, and function. The nucleus exists fully formed; its brevity is a limitation of our macroscopic timescale, not a sign of unreality. You could equally argue that a top quark (lifetime ~\(5 \times 10^{-25}\) seconds) “doesn’t count,” yet we know it’s a fundamental building block of matter. --- ### 5. The periodic table doesn’t care about stability The periodic table is a map of possibilities based on proton count, *not* a census of what lasts. When we fill a box with an element like oganesson (118), we’re saying: “There exists a bound state of 118 protons and some neutrons that, for however long, behaves according to the laws of quantum mechanics and chemistry for that atomic number.” We don’t require it to be stable; we require evidence that it was synthesized and identified. So, to reframe your thought: yes, it’s like a soap bubble that bursts—but before it bursts, it is **perfectly well-defined as