12:25:07 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s the crucial follow‑up, and you’ve put your finger on the practical wall. In theory, you might think: *If we can make element 118, why not just keep going—119, 120, 130, 200…?* The reason we haven’t made “a hundred more” is that **the process rapidly becomes astronomically harder**, and we hit a brick wall of experimental feasibility. Let me break down exactly why the numbers are so small and the progress so slow, even though the basic idea sounds straightforward. --- ### 1. The “straightforward fusion” is actually a game of nuclear dice with awful odds Fusing two nuclei isn’t just a matter of smashing them together. They have to overcome the immense electrostatic repulsion (Coulomb barrier) and then reorganize into a new, heavier bound system that doesn’t immediately fly apart. For “light” synthesis (like making californium from curium and helium), this is doable. For superheavies, the probability of the two nuclei sticking together and then surviving as the desired nucleus is so small that it’s measured in units of **cross‑section** on the order of picobarns (\(10^{-36}\) cm²) or even femtobarns (\(10^{-39}\) cm²). To put that in perspective: - **A picobarn** means that out of **\(10^{12}\)** beam particles hitting the target, roughly **one** might produce a single atom of the new element. - A **femtobarn** (for the heaviest ones) corresponds to one successful event in **\(10^{15}\)** interactions or more. When you run an experiment for months, you may produce **only a handful of atoms—sometimes only one or two**. Every new element after 113 was discovered with single‑digit atom counts. ### 2. We’ve been riding the “calcium‑48 magic bullet,” and we are out of ammo The fastest progress from element 113 to 118 was made using a very special, neutron‑rich isotope: **calcium‑48** (20 protons, 28 neutrons). It’s unusually stable and, when slammed into actinide targets (americium, curium, berkelium, californium), it produced relatively “neutron‑rich” superheavy isotopes that lived long enough to be detected. This was a lucky sweet spot. To go beyond 118, we need a projectile heavier than calcium‑48, because element 119 would require fusing something with 99 protons (einsteinium) with calcium‑48… but einsteinium targets are extremely rare, short‑lived, and difficult to prepare in the required quantities. So researchers are instead trying to use projectiles like **titanium‑50, chromium‑54, iron‑58**, etc., against heavy‑metal targets (e.g., berkelium‑249, californium‑249). These reactions have predicted cross‑sections **10 to 1000 times smaller** than the calcium‑48 reactions. Some are in the femtobarn range, which is at the absolute detection limit of current technology. ### 3. The half‑lives of the products are often too short to detect Even if a fusion event occurs, the resulting nucleus might decay (by alpha emission, spontaneous fission, or electron capture) so quickly—on the order of microseconds—that our detectors can’t see it above background noise. The detection method relies on correlating a series of decays back to the new element. If the first decay happens before the system registers it, or if the chain is broken, the event is lost. For element 119 and beyond, many theoretical models predict ground‑state half‑lives of only tens of microseconds, which pushes the limits of what we can catch. ### 4. Targets and beams are physical nightmares Making a new element isn’t like dialing up a number on an accelerator. You need: - **A target** made of a very rare, often highly radioactive isotope (e.g., berkelium‑249, which has a half‑life of only 330 days and is produced in microgram quantities at just a couple of reactors worldwide). - **An intense, stable beam** of the high‑energy projectile. To get even one atom, you need to bombard the target with trillions to quadrillions of projectiles per second for months. Maintaining that beam intensity without melting the target or breaking the accelerator is a huge engineering challenge. - **A separation and detection array** that can pick out one atom from an enormous avalanche of unwanted reaction products. For elements beyond 118, the required combination of exotic target and projectile often can’t be produced in sufficient quantities, or the cross‑section is so low that even with a year‑long run you’d statistically expect **zero atoms**. ### 5. The “island of stability” remains out of reach Theoretically, there may be superheavy isotopes with longer half‑lives (minutes to years) around neutron number \(N=184\) and proton numbers \(Z=114\)–\(126\). But those are very neutron‑rich. Every projectile and target combination we can realistically make today falls far short of that neutron number. So we are stuck making proton‑rich, short‑lived isotopes that decay fast. That’s why we haven’t just “skipped” to a more stable region: we can’t get there from here. ### 6. It’s not that we aren’t trying—experiments take years You said “only a handful over decades.” Actually, once the calcium‑48 campaign got rolling, we went from element 113 (2004) to 118 (2006) relatively quickly. But attempts to synthesize 119 and 120 have been ongoing for over 15 years at labs in Russia, Germany, Japan, and the US, and so far no confirmed events. The RIKEN lab in Japan, for example, has been running an experiment for element 119 using a vanadium‑51 beam on curium‑248; after hundreds of days of irradiation over several years, they haven’t seen a single atom yet. This illustrates the sheer difficulty. --- **In short:** It’s not that we *can’t* in principle make more elements—it’s that the probability of making them falls off so steeply, and the technical requirements (rare targets, intense beams, detection sensitivity) are now at the absolute frontier. Each new element is a larger mountain to climb, and we are essentially walking a knife‑edge of feasibility. So no, we won’t be popping out element 200 anytime soon; the laws of nuclear physics put a hard, quantitative barrier in front of us that only incremental breakthroughs—new beam facilities, more neutron‑rich projectiles, longer runs—can slowly push back.
